---
title: Who can see what
description: Who can open, upload to and delete in each area of the library, how to share an internal folder, and what "Sensitive" means.
sidebar:
  order: 2
---

What you can do in the library depends on the area, on the folder, and on your role. A folder or file that you cannot open is not shown to you at all — it does not appear in the tree, in the list or in search results.

## Internal Docs

| Where | Tenant admin | Manager | Member |
|---|---|---|---|
| The top level of **Internal Docs**, and folders that are not restricted | Open, upload, rename, delete, share | Open, upload, rename, delete, share | Open and download only |
| A **Restricted** folder | Everything | According to the folder's access list | According to the folder's access list |

A restricted folder shows **Restricted** and the number of people and groups on its list; in the tree it has a lock. Only the people and groups on the list can see it. A folder inside a restricted folder is visible only to people who are on the list of every restricted folder above it.

On an access list, each person or group has one of three levels:

| Level | Allows |
|---|---|
| **Can view** | Open and download. |
| **Can edit** | Also upload, create folders and rename. |
| **Can manage** | Also delete and change who has access (**Share**). |

Tenant admins always have full access.

### Share an internal folder

{% steps %}
1. Open the folder in **Internal Docs** and select **Share**. **Share** is shown if you can manage the folder.
2. Choose who can open it:
   - **Same as "…"** — everyone who can open the folder above can open this one.
   - **Restricted** — only the people and groups you list.
3. For **Restricted**, use **Add people, departments or Partners**, and set **Can view**, **Can edit** or **Can manage** for each. **Expires (optional)** ends someone's access on a date.
4. Select **Save**.
{% /steps %}

You cannot remove your own **Can manage** access. Only folders in **Internal Docs** can be shared.

When you move a file into a folder that a different group of people can see, Operon first shows **Moving changes who can see this document**, with who can see it now and after the move. Select **Move anyway** to go ahead.

## Client Docs

Every user of the firm can open, upload, rename and delete the documents of a client company — unless the company's documents are limited to an access list.

A company limited to an access list has a lock after its name. Then:

- A tenant admin can do everything.
- The people on the company's list can do what the list allows.
- The assignee and the creator of a case for the company can open and edit that case's documents, but not delete them.
- Everyone else sees **Only people on the access list can see these documents.**

The company's access list is kept on the company in **Clients**, not in the library. Ask a tenant admin or the person who manages the company's access if you need it.

## Cases

The **Cases** area lists the cases assigned to you and the cases you created. Who can open a case's files is set out in the case's **Details** › **Privacy** (see [Workspaces and cases](/en/getting-started/workspaces-cases/#the-case-page)): normally the case's department, the managers above the case owner, and tenant admins. The assignee and the creator can upload and edit, but not delete.

## When a button is greyed out

If **Upload** or **New folder** cannot be selected, point at it to see why — for example, the folder is view-only for you, or you are not on the company's access list. Ask the folder's or the company's administrator for access.

## Sensitive documents

Operon gives each file a sensitivity, **HIGH** or **LOW**, from its document type. Files in **Internal Docs**, and files whose type is unknown, are always **HIGH**. Highly sensitive files are marked **Sensitive** in the list and are kept encrypted. You do not set the sensitivity yourself.
